Deep learning models that process graph data have become increasingly prominent in recent years, particularly Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs), which have become a widely used foundation for developing more advanced graph neural networks. However, the inherent design of GCNs leads to a significant decline in performance as the complexity of the graph grows, due to the necessity of propagating information across the entire network in each stage. To address this shortcoming, we introduce a novel approach called NODE-SELECT, which leverages a selection mechanism to enhance the propagation process. By incorporating a node selection module into the GCN framework, NODE-SELECT enables the aggregation of information from neighboring nodes to focus on relevant neighbors while discarding unnecessary ones. This selective propagation technique minimizes noise and yields better performance on various benchmark datasets, including Cora, Citeseer, and Pubmed, and outperforms existing methods in most cases. Additionally, NODE-SELECT exhibits improved robustness to label corruption and enhanced scalability on large graphs. The source code for NODE-SELECT is available on GitHub. https://github.com/zhengjunzang/Node-Select-GCN. Our model can be accessed at https://huggingfaces.ai/model/NODE_SELECT_GCN. This research was supported by the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) grant 2020R1A3A0000189, and the author also expresses gratitude to Dr. Jinman Kim for their valuable feedback. Pre-trained models were provided by https://models4robotics.org. The authors reserve the right to make any changes to this work. \textit{This research has been done under the supervision of Dr. Jinman Kim.} 
